# RFC-0004: Data Access

Status: Active

## Abstract

Application code reads and writes through `IRaythaDbContext`. There is no
repository layer, no second context, and no raw connection outside
`Raytha.Infrastructure`.

## 1. IRaythaDbContext

- Handlers and validators MUST depend on `IRaythaDbContext`, never on
  `RaythaDbContext`. The concrete context exists for EF tooling, migrations, and
  the composition root.
- Every new entity MUST get a `DbSet<T>` on the interface and on the context.
  `PersistenceConventionTests` fails a `DbSet` that exists on only one side.
- `IRaythaDbContext.DbContext` is an escape hatch for EF APIs the interface does
  not expose (change tracking, `Entry`, explicit transactions). Use it
  deliberately and keep it out of query code.
- `Raytha.Web` MUST NOT inject `IRaythaDbContext`. Web code sends a command or
  query through `ISender` (RFC-0002).

## 2. Mapping

- Mapping lives in `src/Raytha.Infrastructure/Persistence/Configurations`, one
  `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` per entity. `PersistenceConventionTests` requires
  each configuration to target a `Raytha.Domain` entity.
- Every configuration MUST be picked up by `ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly`;
  do not configure entities inline in `OnModelCreating`.
- Postgres types are chosen explicitly where it matters: `timestamp with time
  zone` for instants, `jsonb` for field values and other documents, `text` over
  `varchar(n)` unless a length is a real constraint.
- Indexes that a list query depends on MUST be declared in the configuration and
  carried by a migration. A query that sorts or filters on an unindexed column in
  a table that grows (content items, audit logs, webhook deliveries, email logs)
  is a defect.

## 3. Queries

- Read paths MUST be `AsNoTracking()`.
- Project to a DTO in the query (`Select`) rather than materializing entities and
  mapping afterwards. Do not return `IQueryable` out of a handler.
- Paged queries MUST apply `Skip`/`Take` in SQL and MUST count with a separate
  `CountAsync`. Filtering or paging in memory is FORBIDDEN.
- Include only the navigations the response needs. `Include` chains that pull a
  revision graph to render a list are a defect.
- Cancellation tokens MUST be passed to every async EF call.
- Dynamic filtering over custom content-type fields goes through the JSON query
  engine in `Raytha.Infrastructure/JsonQueryEngine`. Hand-built SQL string
  concatenation from user input is FORBIDDEN.

## 4. Writes

- One `SaveChangesAsync(cancellationToken)` per command handler.
- Multi-statement work that must be atomic and cannot be one `SaveChangesAsync`
  uses an explicit transaction through `IRaythaDbContext.DbContext.Database`.
  Nested or overlapping transactions are FORBIDDEN.
- Auditing, timestamps, and soft delete are applied by the context's
  interceptors. Handlers MUST NOT set `CreationTime`, `LastModificationTime`, or
  `IsDeleted` by hand.
- Bulk deletes and updates (`ExecuteDeleteAsync`, `ExecuteUpdateAsync`) bypass
  interceptors and domain events. They are allowed only for maintenance paths
  (log trimming, delivery cleanup) and MUST be commented with why.

## 5. Raw SQL and Dapper

- Raw SQL and Dapper are allowed only inside `Raytha.Infrastructure`, and only
  with parameters. String interpolation of user input into SQL is FORBIDDEN.
- Postgres-specific SQL MUST NOT leak into `Raytha.Application`
  (`LayerDependencyTests` blocks the package references that would let it).

## 6. Migrations

See RFC-0001 §4 for placement and RFC-0012 for naming and the `VERSION` bump. A
migration that changes shape MUST also be reflected in
`db/Postgres/FreshCreateOnLatestVersion.sql` and a `v<old>_to_v<new>.sql` upgrade
script for operators who apply SQL by hand.
